        <div id="core-vs-spike" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Core vs Spike vs Liquid Aeration
          </h2>

          <p>
            Compaction happens when the pore spaces between soil particles are squeezed out by foot traffic, mowers, cars, and heavy rain on bare soil. Roots need those spaces for air and water. When they disappear, water runs off, roots stay shallow, and grass thins in exactly the places people use most.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Core aeration</h3>

          <p>
            A core aerator uses hollow tines or curved spoons to pull plugs of soil, usually 1/2 to 3/4 inch wide and 2 to 3 inches long, and drops them on the surface. Removing soil is the point. The surrounding soil has room to relax into the holes, and the holes give air, water, fertilizer and seed a direct route to the root zone. The plugs break down on the lawn within a couple of weeks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Spike aeration</h3>

          <p>
            A spike aerator pushes a solid spike into the ground. Nothing is removed, so the soil displaced by the spike is pressed into the walls and floor of the hole. On sandy soil that barely matters, and the holes can help seed and water get in. On clay it makes compaction around each hole worse, and the holes close quickly. Spiked lawn shoes are the extreme case: an adult's weight spread over dozens of short spikes rarely penetrates more than an inch in firm soil, and you walk on the lawn to use them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Liquid aeration</h3>

          <p>
            Liquid aerators are soil conditioners and surfactants. They can improve how water moves into tight soil and support soil life, but they do not open channels or remove soil. Think of them as a maintenance product for drainage, not a treatment for compaction.
          </p>

