        <div id="do-you-need-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Does Your Lawn Need Aerating?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every lawn needs aerating every year, and on a healthy lawn with sandy soil it can be a waste of an afternoon. The quickest test costs nothing. Push a long screwdriver into the lawn a day after rain or a good watering. If it slides in 6 inches with little effort, compaction is not your problem. If you have to lean on it to get past an inch or two, the soil is compacted.
          </p>

          <p>
            The lawn gives other signs. Water that puddles or runs off before it soaks in, grass that thins along paths and play areas, and turf that browns quickly in heat even when watered are all consistent with compaction. So is a lawn that responds poorly to fertilizer, because nutrients are not reaching roots. Pull up a small section of sod: roots that stop at an inch or two, often turning sideways, point to hard soil underneath.
          </p>

          <p>
            Soil type sets the baseline. Clay and silty soils compact easily and usually benefit from aeration every year, especially if the lawn gets regular traffic. Loamy lawns with light use may need it every two to three years. Sandy soils rarely compact enough to need it at all. Lawns on new-build lots are a special case: heavy equipment packs the subsoil, and a thin layer of topsoil over it hides the problem. Those lawns almost always benefit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aeration also helps with thatch. The plugs deposited on the surface carry soil microbes that help break thatch down, which is why aeration is often the better first step than aggressive dethatching. If your lawn has a spongy layer thicker than about half an inch, read our guide to the <a href="/best-dethatcher/">best dethatchers</a> as well; the two jobs complement each other.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SCREWDRIVER TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If a screwdriver will not go in 6 inches on moist soil, aerate.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Test in several places, including high-traffic paths and the middle of the lawn. Compaction is often patchy, which is where a manual corer earns its keep.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When to Aerate Cool- and Warm-Season Grass
          </h2>

          <p>
            The rule is simple: aerate when the grass is growing strongly and has several weeks of good growing weather ahead to fill the holes back in. Aerating a dormant or heat-stressed lawn opens it to weeds and drying out with nothing to recover.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cool-season grass</h3>

          <p>
            Kentucky bluegrass, tall fescue, fine fescues and perennial ryegrass grow best in cool weather. For these lawns, early fall is the best time by a clear margin, roughly late August through September in much of the northern half of the US. The soil is still warm, which is ideal for seed germination, nights are cool, and weed pressure is falling. Early spring is the second choice, but aerating then brings up weed seed at the same time crabgrass is getting ready to germinate, and new seed has only a short window before summer heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Warm-season grass</h3>

          <p>
            Bermudagrass, zoysia, St. Augustine and centipede grow in summer heat. Aerate from late spring into early summer, once the lawn has fully greened up and you have mowed it two or three times. That gives it the whole peak growing season to recover. Avoid aerating warm-season grass in fall, when it is heading toward dormancy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two practical timing notes. First, if you use a pre-emergent crabgrass control in spring, aerating after applying it can break the barrier; aerate first, or plan fall aeration instead. Second, plan around seeding. Fall aeration for cool-season lawns works so well partly because it is also the best time to overseed, and seed that falls into aeration holes germinates reliably.
          </p>

