        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Aeration works when it removes soil, on moist ground, at the time of year your grass is growing hardest, followed by seed and fertilizer. For most tractor owners that means a weighted tow-behind plug aerator like the Agri-Fab 45-0299, or the Brinly PA-48BH if you are fighting heavy clay. For small lawns and problem patches, the Yard Butler corer does the same job by hand.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Skip the spike shoes and spike rollers, treat liquids and gypsum as helpers rather than cures, and if your own equipment keeps pulling shallow plugs from clay, rent a gas core aerator for a season. Then put seed down the same day. That combination is what turns a thin, hard lawn into a thick one.
          </p>
